  "transcript": "# The UK’s constitutional safeguards\n\n## The weekly audience and how the monarchy actually works\n> The monarch meets the Prime Minister privately each week to be 'consulted, to encourage and to warn' — a practice that keeps government steady.\n\nChair: Lord Young, welcome. To start: how would you describe the monarch’s role in today’s government?\nLord Young of Old Windsor: Thanks. From working in the Royal Household nearly 20 years, the core is the weekly private audience between the monarch and the Prime Minister — usually an hour after PMQs.\nLord Young of Old Windsor: Before the meeting the Prime Minister talks briefly with the private secretary. The private secretary and I then meet the Prime Minister’s private secretary downstairs. We call it keeping a link between Head of State and Head of Government.\nLord Young of Old Windsor: That hour matters. The PM can speak freely, won’t get quoted on social media, and hears a long-term, non-partisan perspective. It steadies leaders and reduces rash moves.\nLord Burnett of Maldon: So it’s practical, not ceremonial — it has real day-to-day impact?\nLord Young of Old Windsor: Exactly. It’s practical and confidential. The sovereign’s role in that meeting is to be consulted, to encourage and to warn — not to campaign or take sides.\n\n\n## Why confidentiality is vital — and how it sits with public trust\n> Private conversations let leaders be candid; the monarchy builds public trust by acting consistently and representing long-term national interests.\n\nChair: You stress privacy. Is that really essential?\nLord Young of Old Windsor: Absolutely. If those talks were public, ministers couldn’t speak candidly. The Freedom of Information idea of protecting 'formulation of policy' helps — draft thinking needs protection.\nBaroness Hamwee: But people want transparency. How do you build trust if the public can’t see these talks?\nLord Young of Old Windsor: It’s a paradox. You build trust by other visible acts: steady constitutional behaviour, representing the nation, meeting many communities over years. Consistent, non-partisan action over time reassures people.\nBaroness Hamwee: So visibility in public-facing roles balances confidentiality behind closed doors?\nLord Young of Old Windsor: Yes. The monarchy’s long-term perspective — thinking years ahead rather than today’s headlines — helps bridge that trust gap.\n\n\n## When the monarch might act beyond advice — reserve powers\n> There are rare reserve powers the monarch could use in a crisis, for example if there’s uncertainty about who should be Prime Minister or an abuse like asking to prorogue Parliament to avoid losing power.\n\nLord Murphy of Torfaen: Could the monarch ever act as a stronger restraint — more than just giving warnings?\nLord Young of Old Windsor: Yes, in theory. The constitution isn’t all written down; many parts are customs tested over time. Reserve powers exist, for instance over appointing a Prime Minister if it’s unclear who can command the House.\nLord Young of Old Windsor: Another example: the sovereign might refuse a request to prorogue (suspend) Parliament if it was being used to avoid losing power. But those are extreme, last‑resort steps.\nLord Jones of Penybont: Imagine a Government loses a confidence vote but refuses to resign — deadlock. What then?\nLord Young of Old Windsor: It depends on personalities and how rotten the situation is. If the Government truly had no support and the Cabinet collapsed, the sovereign might have to act. But often the system — Cabinet, Parliament, courts — would sort it first.\nLord Burnett of Maldon: Is ambiguity about these powers useful because it lets the system adapt?\nLord Young of Old Windsor: I think so. A rigid written rule can’t predict every crisis. The ambiguity allows experienced advisers to work things out in real time — but it relies on people acting in good faith.\nLord Burnett of Maldon: You gave an example about Boris Johnson and Covid — what happened there?\nLord Young of Old Windsor: When the PM was seriously ill, we had no clear precedent for an in‑office, temporarily incapacitated Prime Minister in a crisis. Officials worked out a temporary plan: the Cabinet Secretary would convene Cabinet to recommend who to appoint briefly if needed. It was ad hoc but effective.\nLord Cryer: Some point to Australia’s 1975 dismissal of Gough Whitlam. Could that happen here?\nLord Young of Old Windsor: Whitlam is a cautionary tale. The present Australian Governor‑General has said she can’t imagine repeating that now. In the UK, I find it hard to see it happening unless the whole government machinery collapsed — unpaid staff, no functioning Cabinet.\n\n\n## Limits of royal intervention and the role of advisers\n> The monarch can’t easily overturn laws a democratically elected Parliament has passed; private secretaries and other officials act as constitutional guardians.\n\nLord Bellamy: Suppose a Government legally imposes press censorship — where does the monarchy stand?\nLord Young of Old Windsor: If a democratically elected government passed such laws after winning an election on that platform, it would be very difficult for the sovereign to stop it. The monarchy isn’t a super‑parliamentary power in those circumstances.\nLord Beith: Is the private secretary to the monarch a guardian of the constitution?\nLord Young of Old Windsor: Yes. That post is one of several constitutional guardians. Private secretaries, the Cabinet Secretary, senior civil servants and judges all help protect constitutional norms.\nBaroness Laing of Elderslie: Has that guardianship changed over time?\nLord Young of Old Windsor: Not really. Predecessors I spoke to from the 1950s onwards followed similar principles: try to anticipate problems and prevent crises before they become public.\n\n\n## Practical safeguards and what could be improved\n> The system is resilient but not perfect: keep vigilance, update the Cabinet Manual, and teach people earlier about how the constitution actually works.\n\nChair: Do you think our constitutional safeguards are sufficient?\nLord Young of Old Windsor: I don’t want to sound complacent. From my experience, I see no clearly better system than ours, but it depends on people acting in good faith. We should stay vigilant.\nLord Young of Old Windsor: Two practical steps: update the Cabinet Manual so it reflects recent practice, and improve education so people understand our uncodified constitution earlier in life.\nLord Jones of Penybont: If a determined Government controls Parliament, are any safeguards left?\nLord Young of Old Windsor: If a majority Government legitimately passes laws, it’s hard to see constitutional bodies overturning that. The trouble is when democratic outcome and public perception diverge because of electoral quirks.\nChair: Thank you, Lord Young. Any final point?\nLord Young of Old Windsor: Only that the system’s strengths are the human relationships, the careful, confidential advice and shared practices. Keep those strong, teach the basics, and refresh guidance documents like the Cabinet Manual.\n",
